# leadingit.me — Apache 2.4 / cPanel (HostGator shared, addon domain, behind Cloudflare)
# Owned by deploy-engineer. Vite copies this file into dist/ verbatim (public/ passthrough).
# NO SPA catch-all: every route is a real prerendered file (vite-react-ssg, dirStyle: nested).
# There is deliberately no `RewriteRule ^ /index.html` anywhere in this file.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Canonicalisation: HTTPS + apex host, single hop, addon-domain-safe
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Cloudflare (SSL mode Full/strict) always connects to this origin over HTTPS, so %{HTTPS}
# alone would never see plain http from behind the proxy — X-Forwarded-Proto carries the
# client's real scheme. Both conditions must be true (AND, the default — no [OR] flag) before
# redirecting, otherwise a Flexible-SSL-style proxy hop (HTTPS off at origin, XFP: https from
# the edge) would bounce forever.
#
# Target host is hardcoded to the literal string "leadingit.me" (never %{HTTP_HOST}) and the
# path comes from the RewriteRule's own capture ($1), never %{REQUEST_URI}. This matters
# because leadingit.me is a cPanel ADDON domain whose docroot is a subdirectory inside the
# primary account's docroot tree, so this same .htaccess is also reached when someone requests
# the primary domain with a "/leadingit.me/<path>" prefix (the duplicate-content trap). In that
# case mod_rewrite strips the physical per-directory prefix before matching the RewriteRule
# pattern, so $1 is already the correct bare path ("about/"), while %{REQUEST_URI} would still
# carry the leaked "/leadingit.me/" prefix and produce a broken double-prefixed redirect target.
RewriteEngine On

# Force HTTPS (proxy-aware, loop-safe)
RewriteCond %{HTTPS} !=on
RewriteCond %{HTTP:X-Forwarded-Proto} !=https
RewriteRule ^(.*)$ https://leadingit.me/$1 [R=301,L,QSA]

# Canonical host: apex only. Catches "www.", the addon-domain duplicate-content path
# (any HTTP_HOST that isn't the apex — including the primary domain's tree), and any other
# host/IP this vhost happens to answer for.
RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} !^leadingit\.me$ [NC]
RewriteRule ^(.*)$ https://leadingit.me/$1 [R=301,L,QSA]

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Retired routes → 301 (link equity, never a 404)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two things, and only two things, retire a route. (a) Its subject stops existing at the
# manufacturer — the S Series case below. (b) The commercial basis a locked route was planned on
# turns out not to exist, so the page could only generate enquiries the business cannot service —
# the shading case below. Neither is "the copy is thin" and neither is "the page is being
# rewritten"; those are reasons to fix a page, not to retire a URL. Each entry records what was
# retired, why, and when, because a redirect with no reason attached is the one thing nobody
# dares delete later.
#
# These sit BELOW the scheme/host block on purpose. Placed above it, a retired URL requested on
# http:// or www. would jump straight to the canonical apex target in one hop — marginally
# shorter, but it would make retired URLs the only paths on the site that skip canonicalisation,
# a special case future maintainers would have to rediscover. Below it, a retired URL behaves
# exactly like every other URL here: canonicalise first, then resolve. Every real inbound link
# and every Googlebot fetch already arrives at https://leadingit.me, so the normal path is a
# single 301 to the target; only a legacy http/www link pays a second hop, and it pays that on
# every page of the site, not just this one.
#
# They are also above the dot-directory deny block and the DirectorySlash section deliberately.
# Once a route is retired its directory no longer exists in dist/, so mod_dir's DirectorySlash
# cannot fire for the slashless form (it only redirects to real directories) — the `/?$` in the
# pattern is what handles the non-slashed form, and it must be reached before the request falls
# through to ErrorDocument 404. Nothing above touches /.well-known/ or the AutoSSL exemption.
#
# 2026-07-31 — /brands/uandksound/s-series/ (Muneeb, OQ #21). All three S Series models
# (S6 I, S6 II, S1200 I) are listed under Discontinued Products on uandksound.com and the
# series is absent from its live collections tree, so the page had no current subject left.
# Target is the brand hub, which is the closest surviving intent (uandksound loudspeakers) —
# not /brands/uandksound/m-series/, which is a different series and would be a misleading match.
# NOTE: /brands/uandksound/m-series/ is NOT retired and must not be added here — M4500D is
# still current and that route is deliberately retained (docs/05-URL-TAXONOMY.md §Migrations).
RewriteRule ^brands/uandksound/s-series/?$ https://leadingit.me/brands/uandksound/ [R=301,L,QSA]

# 2026-07-31 — /solutions/shading/ (Muneeb, OQ #22: "drop shading for now"). There is no shading
# product in the catalogue — no motor, track, tubular drive, blind or curtain system across any of
# the nine brands. The only thing supporting the page was the Crestron DIN-2MC2 motor-control
# module, which makes it an integration-only page competing for supply queries ("motorised curtains
# dubai") the business cannot fulfil. Target is the solutions index: the honest parent, one level
# up, category-level intent with no shading promise attached. Deliberately NOT
# /solutions/whole-home-control/ or /solutions/lighting-control/ — sending shading supply intent to
# a page that also cannot supply shading just moves the disappointment and buys traffic dishonestly.
# REVERSIBLE ("for now"): if a shading product enters the catalogue, or a motor/track supplier is
# confirmed and nameable, delete this rule and return the route to the manifest with the same slug.
# ORDERING NOTE: /solutions/ is locked in docs/05 §2 but not built yet (docs/04 §15) — like the
# retired route itself it emits no HTML today, so this rule cannot fire on anything reachable until
# the Phase 4 solutions template ships, at which point the target resolves 200. If /solutions/ is
# ever dropped as well, this target moves to / in the same change; a 301 to a 404 is not acceptable
# once either URL is live.
RewriteRule ^solutions/shading/?$ https://leadingit.me/solutions/ [R=301,L,QSA]

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Legacy WordPress URLs → 301  (added 2026-08-06)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# leadingit.me served a WordPress site until this build replaced it. Enumerated from its own
# live wp-sitemap.xml on 2026-08-06: 35 indexable URLs, no noindex, self-referential canonicals
# — so they are crawlable and may be indexed and linked.
#
# TIMING, and it is not the obvious one: these break on the FIRST deploy, not at prune. This
# file replaces WordPress's own .htaccess, and WP pretty permalinks exist only because of the
# `RewriteRule . /index.php [L]` in that file. The moment ours lands, every WP URL 404s even
# though the PHP files are still sitting there. So these rules ship WITH the cutover deploy.
#
# Only the three URLs with a genuine equivalent are redirected. The other 32 — six 2017-2019
# posts, a portfolio item, /blog/, a category, an author archive and 20 tag archives — are
# commercial-AV content (auditoriums, classrooms, Skype-for-Business rooms) for a line of
# business this site does not serve. Sending them to a residential solutions page would be a
# mismatch dressed as a redirect, which is the soft-404 pattern Google devalues and the
# doorway-adjacent behaviour CLAUDE.md rule 6 forbids. They are left to ErrorDocument 404
# deliberately. See docs/07-DEPLOYMENT-HOSTGATOR.md §1a for the full inventory and the reasoning.
RewriteRule ^products/?$ https://leadingit.me/brands/ [R=301,L,QSA]
RewriteRule ^locations-2/?$ https://leadingit.me/locations/dubai/ [R=301,L,QSA]
RewriteRule ^main-under-upgrade/?$ https://leadingit.me/ [R=301,L,QSA]

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Parent paths that are not pages → 301
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Not a retirement — /locations/ never existed and never will. docs/05-URL-TAXONOMY.md §2a is
# explicit: "No index page while there is one location. 301 → /locations/dubai/ so a truncated
# URL never 404s." Someone hand-editing a URL, or a crawler walking up the path, must land on
# the one location page rather than on ErrorDocument 404.
#
# Same placement reasoning as the retired routes above: below the scheme/host block so the URL
# canonicalises first, and above DirectorySlash — /locations/ is not a directory in dist/
# (only /locations/dubai/ is), so mod_dir cannot help and the `/?$` here handles both forms.
#
# This rule is what makes it safe for /locations/dubai/ to carry a two-crumb breadcrumb
# (Home → Dubai): there is no /locations/ page for a middle crumb to point at, by design.
# NEVER add a second /locations/<city>/ route — docs/05 §5a: a city-swap location page for a
# city with no premises is a doorway page, and that includes every Pakistani city, permanently.
RewriteRule ^locations/?$ https://leadingit.me/locations/dubai/ [R=301,L,QSA]

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Trailing slash / directory index
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Canonical URLs always carry a trailing slash (docs/05-URL-TAXONOMY.md). dirStyle "nested"
# emits one real file per route as <route>/index.html. For the canonical slashed URL
# (e.g. /about/) DirectoryIndex resolves the file with NO redirect at all — zero rules needed.
# For the non-slash form (e.g. /about), Apache's own mod_dir DirectorySlash issues exactly one
# 301 straight to the slashed URL — this is native behaviour, not something we re-implement, so
# it can never chain with itself. (It can only ever add a second hop on top of the scheme/host
# rules above, and only for the edge case of a non-canonical host/scheme requested WITHOUT a
# trailing slash in the same request — not a path any real link on the site produces.)
#
# That last claim is enforced in the app, not assumed: every <a href>/<Link to> renders through
# href() in src/seo/paths.ts, which emits the slashed form, so no internal link on the site ever
# lands on DirectorySlash. Only external links and typed URLs reach the 301. If a future change
# starts emitting slashless internal hrefs, this comment becomes false and ~2,000 internal
# clicks per crawl become redirect hops — the fix belongs in href(), never in a rewrite here.
DirectoryIndex index.html
Options -Indexes -MultiViews

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Real 404
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ErrorDocument 404 /404.html

# Deny access to dotfiles (.env, .git*, etc.) and any stray source maps if ever emitted.
<FilesMatch "^\.|\.map$">
  Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

# FilesMatch above only ever tests the request's BASENAME, so it cannot see a dot that
# is on a DIRECTORY instead of the final segment: /.vite/ssr-manifest.json has basename
# "ssr-manifest.json" (no leading dot) even though the directory is dot-prefixed, and
# sails straight through the rule above. `npm run build`'s postbuild step
# (scripts/clean-build-artifacts.mjs) already deletes Vite's dist/.vite/ manifests
# before anything is uploaded, so this should never be reachable in practice — this is
# the second layer of "belt and braces" in case a future build config or a manual
# upload reintroduces a dot-prefixed directory (.vite/, .git/, .svn/, etc). Path-level,
# so it must be checked against the full REQUEST_URI via mod_rewrite, not FilesMatch.
# /.well-known/ is exempted: cPanel AutoSSL's HTTP domain-control validation writes a
# token file under /.well-known/pki-validation/ (and ACME clients under
# /.well-known/acme-challenge/) that must stay publicly fetchable or certificate
# issuance/renewal breaks.
<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
  RewriteCond %{REQUEST_URI} !^/\.well-known/
  RewriteCond %{REQUEST_URI} (^|/)\.
  RewriteRule ^ - [F,L]
</IfModule>

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Compression
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
<IfModule mod_deflate.c>
  AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE text/html text/plain text/css text/xml text/javascript
  AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE application/javascript application/json application/xml
  AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE application/xhtml+xml image/svg+xml font/ttf font/otf
  AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE application/vnd.ms-fontobject
  # Not deflating: woff2/avif/webp/jpg/png — already compressed, deflate wastes CPU on these.
</IfModule>

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Cache headers
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
<IfModule mod_expires.c>
  ExpiresActive On
  ExpiresByType text/html "access plus 0 seconds"
  ExpiresDefault "access plus 1 week"
</IfModule>

<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
  # Flag hashed Vite build output (/assets/*) for the immutable long-cache rule below.
  # Only /assets/* is content-hashed; unhashed static files must NOT get an immutable
  # 1-year TTL. That includes the committed product/brand derivatives under /products/
  # and /brands/, which scripts/build-images.mjs emits at stable paths — a stale hero
  # would be cached for a year with no way to bust it.
  RewriteRule ^assets/ - [E=ASSET_CACHE:1]
</IfModule>

<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable" env=ASSET_CACHE
  <FilesMatch "\.html$">
    Header set Cache-Control "no-cache, must-revalidate"
  </FilesMatch>
  # Fonts are served with crossorigin="anonymous" in the HTML (see app CSS/JS preloads);
  # without an explicit ACAO the browser can silently refuse the cached copy.
  <FilesMatch "\.(woff2?|ttf|otf|eot)$">
    Header set Access-Control-Allow-Origin "https://leadingit.me"
  </FilesMatch>
</IfModule>

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Security headers
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  Header always set X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
  Header always set Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
  # Modest max-age while HSTS is newly rolled out; ramp up over time. Deliberately NO
  # includeSubDomains and NO preload: the zone carries non-web subdomain records that
  # must never be forced to HTTPS-only.
  Header always set Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=86400" env=HTTPS
  Header always set X-Frame-Options "SAMEORIGIN"
  # The remote stock-photo img-src exemption was removed on 2026-07-30, when the Phase 3
  # image pipeline (scripts/build-images.mjs) landed and the last of the 26 hotlinks went.
  # Nothing under src/ references a remote image host any more and every image is served
  # from this origin, so img-src is now 'self' plus data:. Do not re-add a remote image
  # origin: an image we cannot serve ourselves is one we have no licence trail for
  # (CLAUDE.md asset rules / ORCHESTRATOR-BRIEF.md §5).
  # script-src/style-src include 'unsafe-inline': vite-react-ssg injects small inline
  # <script> hydration-data tags and Tailwind/framer-motion emit inline styles — both are
  # build output, not user input, but this is a known CSP weakening. A stricter nonce- or
  # hash-based policy needs a build-time change (out of scope here; app code) — flagged for
  # frontend-engineer / Phase 2.
  # Two third-party origins are allowed, and ONLY these two. Both were added in Phase 5
  # because the code that needs them shipped inert behind an empty key, so nothing failed
  # in testing and the omission was invisible until QA read the emitted header:
  #   - challenges.cloudflare.com — Cloudflare Turnstile on /contact/. Needs script-src to
  #     load the widget, frame-src because the widget renders in an iframe (there was no
  #     frame-src directive at all, so it fell back to default-src 'self' and was blocked),
  #     and connect-src for its own token exchange.
  #   - googletagmanager.com + google-analytics.com — GA4. script-src loads gtag.js;
  #     connect-src is where the measurement hits actually go.
  # Without these the "paste one key and it works" contract in src/lib/turnstile.ts and
  # src/lib/analytics.ts is false: pasting a Turnstile site key alone would have switched
  # /contact/ from a working mailto form to one that can never submit, failing only in the
  # browser console. Do not remove an origin here without removing the feature that needs it.
  Header always set Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://challenges.cloudflare.com https://www.googletagmanager.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https://www.googletagmanager.com; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://challenges.cloudflare.com https://www.googletagmanager.com https://*.google-analytics.com https://*.analytics.google.com; frame-src https://challenges.cloudflare.com; frame-ancestors 'self'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; object-src 'none'"
  # ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Header unset X-Powered-By
</IfModule>
